; R7RS 6.2.6 (`/`): "It is an error if any argument other than the first is an
; exact zero." An INEXACT zero is NOT an error — it is ordinary IEEE-754
; division, so the result is an infinity or a NaN, and inexactness is
; contagious, so a BIGNUM over an inexact zero is an inexact infinity.
;
; Native was the wrong side here (was found/bignum_div_inexact_zero_native.esk,
; promoted to the corpus by the fix): the bignum runtime's mixed-mode division
; carried a `(rd != 0.0) ? ld / rd : 0.0` guard, so the dividend was dropped
; entirely and the answer was a silent exact 0 — the worst possible shape for
; a numeric defect, since 0 is a plausible value.
;
; The exact-zero divisor stays fatal on both substrates and is gated by
; tests/vm_parity/fatal/exact_div_zero.esk and fatal/bignum_div_zero.esk.
(display (/ (expt 2 100) 0.0)) (newline)
(display (/ (- (expt 2 100)) 0.0)) (newline)
(display (/ (expt 2 100) 2.0)) (newline)
(display (/ 0.0 0.0)) (newline)
